In practice, the Gaussian PDF $f_X$ is truncated to the domain $[0,1]$ and re-normalized such that $\int_0^1 f_X(s)\,ds = 1$. Additionally, a uniform floor $\varepsilon$ is blended into the distribution:
\begin{align}
    f_X(s) \leftarrow (1-\varepsilon)\,f_X(s) + \varepsilon
\end{align}
followed by re-normalization. This prevents $f_X(s) \approx 0$ near the boundaries of $[0,1]$, which would otherwise cause $\phi'(\tau) = \frac{t_f}{2f_X(t_{norm})}$ (see \autoref{eq:deriv}) to become arbitrarily large and introduce numerical ill-conditioning into the NLP. In this work, $\varepsilon = 0.05$ is used.

As $\sigma \rightarrow \infty$, the Gaussian PDF becomes:
\begin{align}
    f_X(s) = \frac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi}}\exp{\left( \frac{-(s-\mu_\text{PDF})^2}{2\sigma^2}\right)}\rightarrow \frac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi}}
\end{align}
since the exponent $\frac{-(s-\mu_\text{PDF})^2}{2\sigma^2}\rightarrow0$ for any fixed $s$. After truncation and re-normalization to $[0, 1]$:
\begin{align}
    f_X(s) = \frac{1/\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}{\int_0^1 1/\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}\,ds} = 1
\end{align}
So $f_X(s)\rightarrow 1$ uniformly on $[0, 1]$. The corresponding CDF is $F_X(s) = s$, and substituting into 
\eqref{eq:transform}:
\begin{align}
    \phi(\tau) = t_f F_X^{-1}\left(\frac{\tau+1}{2}\right) = 
    t_f\cdot\frac{\tau+1}{2}
\end{align}
which is the affine transformation \autoref{eq:affine} with $t_0=0$.
